 "observations": [
  "The page number «155» stands at the head of the page, centred, with a rule below it; at the right the number of the table, «Tab. 44», in bold sans serif, underlined. At the foot, the signature «20*» on the right; recorded here, not transcribed as a line. The titles (L01-L04), in bold, the sigla in roman; L03, C's title «Tadil almareh», as on p. 153 (JP2 0185); L04, «Locusque1) draconis eius 0 S. 21 G. 54 M.», in smaller bold type, followed by «(CN)». The folios of the manuscripts stand at the right of L03 and L04, «C 64v, O 122r,» and «N 18r.», the letters raised; here as right margin notes.",
  "The table: rules and heads as on p. 154 (JP2 0186), with «Pagina numerorum (O) Semita numeri (C)» over the four columns of the arguments, divided below a rule into «Augmentum proprietatum omnium (ON)» and «Diminutio proprietatnm omnium (ON)» in smaller type, the sigla in place of a note; «Sublimatio ascensus2) Martis examinata3)»; «Examinatio ipsius elheza4)» and «Examinatio centri», each hyphenated «Exami-natio»; «Statio prima Martis»; «Latitudo Martis descendentis5)» over «prima» and «secunda» in smaller bold type. Here each head is set from the top, one printed line to a line (L05-L09), the cells separated by our «|»; a head followed by empty cells spans their columns. L10, the units, in bold.",
  "L11-L40. Checked as on p. 150 (JP2 0182): the Sublimatio is 4s 8° 24′ less half the Examinatio argumenti in every row, a half minute counted as a whole, except at 173° and 176° (L33, L36), where the sign and the degrees are misprinted: see the critical notes. It passes into sign 4 at 171° and is 4s 8° 24′, the apogee itself, at 180°. The Latitudo secunda is 3° 45′ · sin x to within 0.5″ in every row, and its value for 180° − y is that of y on p. 150 in every row.",
  "L11-L40, the Examinatio argumenti. The radius that reproduces each value as the equation of an epicycle falls from 38.84 at 151° to 38.76 at 160°-163° and rises to 39.56 at 179°; near 180° the equation changes fast with the radius, so that against the epicycle of radius 39 the table is up to 23.2′ smaller at 160°-161° (28° 23′ at 161° for 28° 46.2′) and up to 7.8′ larger at 177°. The steps of the column grow from 33′ to 116′ a degree, regularly except between 164° and 165° (70′, where 67′ and 76′ stand on either side).",
  "L11-L40, the other columns: the Examinatio centri lies within 0.9′ of 11° 10′ · sin x from 151° to 162° and from 165° to 180° within 1.8′ below it, and 2.1′ and 2.3′ above it at 163° and 164° (3° 18′ and 3° 7′), so that it falls by 14′ from 164° to 165°; its values for 180° − y are those of y on p. 150 (JP2 0182) at 151° and from 165° on (except 1′ at 179°), and 1′ to 4′ larger from 153° to 164°, where p. 150 lies up to 2.2′ below 11° 10′ · sin x. The Statio prima increases from 5s 18° 24′ to 5s 19° 15′ at 180°, its greatest value: by 3′ or 4′ a degree to 157°, irregularly by 1′ to 3′ to 162°, by 2′ to 168° and then by 1′ or not at all. The Latitudo prima decreases from 0° 43′ at 151° to 0° 27′ at 178°-180°, its least value, by 1′ in two degrees out of three to 167° and then every second or third degree.",
  "Notes on the readings. Note a: the last argument, 6s 0°, is C's; O has 5. Checked: the Examinatio centri 5° 14′ at 152° (note b, O and N, against C's 24) is the value of 28° on p. 150 (JP2 0182); the Latitudo prima 0° 32′ at 167° (note d, C, against O's 22) fits its column; the Latitudo secunda 1° 24′ 17″ and 1° 16′ 57″ at 158° and 160° (notes e and f, C), 1° 24′ 17.2″ and 1° 16′ 57.3″ computed, and 1° 13′ 15″ at 161° (note g), the edition's 15 against the 35 of C and O, 1° 13′ 15.2″ computed and the value of p. 150 at 19°; the note does not say «correxi». At 177° (note c) the Statio prima has C's 5s 19° 13′, the value of 176°, where O has 14, the value of 178°; the column does not decide between them.",
  "Italics: «om.». N03: a speck above the C of «57] C,»; not transcribed. L08, «proprietatnm», L33 and L36: see the critical notes."
 ],
